const faqs = [
  {
    question: 'What purity of potassium humate are these 30-crop dosage rates calculated for?',
    answer:
      'All solid soil and drip rates in this master matrix assume a concentrated, water-soluble KOH-extracted Potassium Humate powder or flake containing 70%–80% total humic substances (ISO 19822 verified). If you use a 12%–15% liquid humic concentrate, multiply the solid weight rate by roughly 5x to 6x to deliver an equivalent humic dose.',
  },
  {
    question: 'Why does the foliar column specify Potassium Fulvate instead of Potassium Humate?',
    answer:
      'Fulvic acid has a low molecular weight (<2,000 Da) that crosses leaf cuticles efficiently without leaving dark, opaque deposits on foliage or clogging fine spray nozzles. It also remains fully soluble across pH 1–14, preventing tank-mix precipitation with mildly acidic amino acids or micronutrient chelates.',
  },
  {
    question: 'How do I convert Per Hectare rates accurately to Per Acre?',
    answer:
      'One hectare equals 2.471 acres. Divide any Per Hectare rate (kg/ha) by 2.471 to get the Per Acre rate (kg/ac)—or click the "Per Acre" toggle above the table to switch all 30 crop rows instantaneously.',
  },
  {
    question: 'Can I apply the full seasonal humic dose in one single drip irrigation event?',
    answer:
      'No—especially in sandy or low-organic soils. Splitting potassium humate into 1.0–2.0 kg/ha drip pulses every 14 to 21 days during key physiological windows (root establishment, tillering/branching, pre-flowering) maintains higher rhizosphere bioavailability and reduces leaching.',
  },
];

